from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "5. 2 Thessalonians 1:7–10"),
("p", "Claim: Paul’s words on “everlasting destruction from the presence of the Lord” are read in the sentence that carries them, and that sentence is judicial. It speaks of a penalty paid, set against a rest given to the saints, and it names an exclusion from the Lord’s presence that does not end. The preposition alone does not carry the doctrine, and this chapter does not ask it to."),
("sub", "5.1 The setting"),
("p", "The letter is written “unto the church of the Thessalonians in God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ” (2 Thessalonians 1:1). Its readers are under pressure. Paul thanks God for their faith and tells them that he glories in them “in the churches of God for your patience and faith in all your persecutions and tribulations that ye endure” (2 Thessalonians 1:4). He then explains what their endurance shows: “Which is a manifest token of the righteous judgment of God, that ye may be counted worthy of the kingdom of God, for which ye also suffer” (2 Thessalonians 1:5). The sentence that holds the verses in question begins in the next verse, and what it says is bound up with that setting. It is a word of comfort to persecuted saints about the justice of God toward those who persecute them."),
("p", "The passage is read as written. It tells the saints at Thessalonica what will be done to those who trouble them, and it is not a warning addressed to the Body of Christ about its own standing. Its subject is the end of persecutors and of those who know not God, and the volume treats it as a witness to that end."),
("sub", "5.2 One sentence"),
("p", "Verses 6 to 10 are a single sentence in the King James Version, and its parts have to be read together. The sentence says that “it is a righteous thing with God to recompense tribulation to them that trouble you”, and that He will give rest to the troubled “when the Lord Jesus shall be revealed from heaven with his mighty angels, In flaming fire taking vengeance on them that know not God, and that obey not the gospel of our Lord Jesus Christ: Who shall be punished with everlasting destruction from the presence of the Lord, and from the glory of his power; When he shall come to be glorified in his saints, and to be admired in all them that believe” (2 Thessalonians 1:6–10)."),
("tbl", "The parts of the sentence.", [
    ("2 Thessalonians 1:6", T("2Thess 1:6"), "The ground: it is a righteous thing with God to recompense tribulation to those who trouble the saints."),
    ("2 Thessalonians 1:7", T("2Thess 1:7"), "The saints who are troubled are given rest with Paul, when the Lord Jesus is revealed from heaven with His mighty angels."),
    ("2 Thessalonians 1:8", T("2Thess 1:8"), "The Lord comes in flaming fire, taking vengeance on those who know not God and obey not the gospel."),
    ("2 Thessalonians 1:9", T("2Thess 1:9"), "These shall be punished with everlasting destruction from the presence of the Lord and from the glory of His power."),
    ("2 Thessalonians 1:10", T("2Thess 1:10"), "The same coming is the Lord glorified in His saints and admired in all them that believe."),
  ], [0.2, 0.45, 0.35]),
("p", "The vocabulary is the vocabulary of a court. There is a “righteous judgment” (verse 5), a “recompense” (verse 6), a “vengeance” taken (verse 8) and a punishment (verse 9). The verb in verse 9 is the verb of a penalty paid: those named will be punished, that is, will pay the penalty. The destruction is what the penalty is. This governs how it is read. A penalty is exacted by a judge. It is a thing that is done to persons for what they have done, and the sentence says in what it consists and from what the persons are removed."),
("sub", "5.3 What “destruction” does in this sentence"),
("p", "The noun used here is not the one of Matthew 10:28 and Philippians 3:19. It is the word that Paul uses in the first letter, “sudden destruction cometh upon them, as travail upon a woman with child; and they shall not escape” (1 Thessalonians 5:3), and in the letter to Timothy of lusts “which drown men in destruction and perdition” (1 Timothy 6:9). It is also the word of the sentence on the offender at Corinth, who is to be delivered “unto Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus” (1 Corinthians 5:5). In that last verse the person who is destroyed in the flesh is the same person whose spirit is saved, and he is alive when the sentence is passed and alive when it is carried out. The word there names a heavy loss inflicted on a living man, and the man does not cease."),
("p", "The point is the same as in chapter 3. The noun can name an ending, as when a thing is broken beyond use, and it can name a ruin inflicted on one who goes on. Nothing in the word itself says which is meant at 2 Thessalonians 1:9, and the writer who holds that the lost cease to exist is right that the word is available to his reading. The sentence has to be read for what it adds."),
("sub", "5.4 What the sentence adds"),
("p", "It adds three things. The first is the adjective. The destruction is “everlasting”. The volume takes up the word at length in chapter 6 and concedes there that it can describe a result as well as a process. Here it is enough to say that the sentence does not describe a destruction that is accomplished and is over. A penalty that is called everlasting is a penalty whose effect does not end and is not reversed. That much is Stated."),
("p", "The second is the contrast with the saints. The same sentence says that the saints will have “rest” when the Lord is revealed (verse 7), and that the Lord is “glorified in his saints” and “admired in all them that believe” at His coming (verse 10). The persons who are punished are described by what they are away from: “from the presence of the Lord, and from the glory of his power” (verse 9). The presence and the glory are the place where the saints are given rest and the Lord is admired. The sentence sets the two fates side by side, and the second is the loss of what the first receives. That is Shown by the context."),
("p", "The third is the Lord’s own use of the same idea. In the Gospels the lost are not only those who are removed but those who are shut out. To those who plead their works the Lord says, “I never knew you: depart from me, ye that work iniquity” (Matthew 7:23). Of the virgins who came late it is said that “the door was shut” and the answer was “I know you not” (Matthew 25:10, 12). In Luke the master of the house “hath shut to the door”, those outside stand and knock, and the answer is “depart from me, all ye workers of iniquity”, and there is “weeping and gnashing of teeth, when ye shall see Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob, and all the prophets, in the kingdom of God, and you yourselves thrust out” (Luke 13:25–28). These passages are spoken to Israel in the Kingdom ministry and are not applied to the Body. They are used for what they reveal about the wicked: the condition they describe is a condition of exclusion, and in the last of them the persons shut out see what they are shut out from."),
("sub", "5.5 “From the presence of the Lord”"),
("p", "The clause in question reads “from the presence of the Lord, and from the glory of his power”. The preposition can be read in two ways. It can name the place from which the lost are removed, so that the penalty is a casting away from the Lord. It can name the source, so that the destruction comes forth from the Lord’s presence as the earth and the heaven “fled away” from the face of Him that sat on the throne (Revelation 20:11). The writer who holds that the lost cease to exist may take either reading and find his view in it, and the writer who holds that they continue may do the same. The volume does not rest the case on the preposition."),
("p", "It rests on the setting, and the setting favours the first reading without requiring it. The sentence has been speaking of rest for the troubled saints, and of the Lord glorified in them. The persons it turns to have been described as those who “know not God” and “obey not the gospel” (2 Thessalonians 1:8). The penalty named for them is a destruction that does not end, from the presence of the One whom the saints are with. Read so, the verse states an enduring exclusion. The wording of the clause has a likeness in the prophet’s picture of the day of the Lord, in which men go “into the holes of the rocks, and into the caves of the earth, for fear of the LORD, and for the glory of his majesty, when he ariseth to shake terribly the earth” (Isaiah 2:19). The volume notes the likeness of idea and does not claim that Paul is quoting."),
("p", "The verse does not describe the experience of those excluded. It does not use the word torment, and the volume does not say that it states continuing consciousness. It states a penalty that is everlasting, a destruction, and an exclusion from the presence of the Lord. Whether the penalty includes the experience of being under it is a question for the passages that speak of it directly, and the volume turns to them in chapters 7 and 8. A reader who says that the verse is consistent with the annihilation of the persons has said what is true: it is consistent with it. The volume’s reply is that the verse is also consistent with the persons’ continuing, that it does not decide between the two, and that the verses which do decide are read in their places."),
("ctbl", "What is stated, shown and inferred in the passage.", [
    ("Point", "How the volume holds it", "Mark"),
    ("The penalty is everlasting", "The adjective is set on the destruction", "Stated"),
    ("The sentence is judicial", "Judgment, recompense, vengeance and punishment are the words used", "Stated"),
    ("The lost are set over against the saints", "Rest and glory for the saints, exclusion from the presence for the lost", "Shown by the context"),
    ("The exclusion is enduring", "Read from the adjective, the judicial words and the Gospels’ shut door", "Inferred"),
    ("The persons continue to be", "Not stated; argued from other passages, not from this one", "Not claimed here"),
    ("The persons cease to be", "Not stated; the verse is consistent with it and does not require it", "Not claimed here"),
  ], [0.28, 0.5, 0.22]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "The passage is addressed to a persecuted assembly and speaks of the end of those who trouble them and know not God; it is not a warning to the Body.",
    "Verses 6 to 10 are one judicial sentence, and the destruction is the penalty the Lord exacts.",
    "The noun for destruction is used elsewhere of a loss inflicted on a living man, so the word alone does not decide.",
    "The penalty is everlasting and is set against the rest and glory of the saints, which is the sentence’s own contrast.",
    "The Gospels’ shut door and the command to depart describe an exclusion in which the persons are said to see what they are shut out from.",
    "The preposition is read as an enduring exclusion from the setting, not from the preposition; the verse does not state whether the persons continue to experience it.",
  ]),
]
